{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260614195230d51fa08e5b014bcd","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me implement the PI session-tree fallback in detectFork. Looking at the PI types, I need to use ctx.sessionManager.getEntries() to get session entries and find the fork point from the session tree.\n\nThe fallback logic should:\n1. Read session entries from ctx.sessionManager.getEntries()\n2. Find the parent session (the one we're forking from)\n3. Determine the fork point (the last entry in the parent session)\n4. Return fork info with the source thread ref and fork entryId\n\nHowever, there's a challenge: the source thread ref is an LHC thread reference, not a PI session reference. In Epic 1, we're in observe-only mode, so PI still runs its own session. The LHC thread for the current session should have been captured in the connector's state.\n\nActually, looking at the issue more carefully:\n- The session_before_fork hook provides the fork point\n- The Epic 1 fallback is when this hook is absent (or not fired)\n- In that case, we need to use PI's session tree (ctx.sessionManager.getEntries()) to detect the fork\n\nThe challenge is that without the hook, we don't have an explicit \"fork\" signal. We need to infer it from:\n1. The session entries showing a parent-child relationship\n2. The fact that we're starting a new session after an existing one\n\nFor Epic 1, the connector's state should hold the current thread ref (from the previous session). So the fallback can use that as the source thread ref.\n\nBut there's another issue: the fork entryId. Without the hook, we don't know the exact entryId of the fork point. We'd need to infer it from the session entries or use the last captured event's position.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The story says:\n- Fork detection using `session_before_fork` when present, with PI's session tree as Epic 1 fallback evidence\n\nThe key phrase is \"as Epic 1 fallback evidence\" - the session tree is EVIDENCE for the fork, not the primary detection mechanism. \n\nWhen the hook is unavailable, I'll use the session tree as evidence to detect a fork. The `previousSessionFile` in `session_start` provides a clear indicator of a new session after an existing one. I'll use the last captured event's position or entry from the session entries to determine the fork point. The implementation will involve checking the session_start hook for evidence and using the session tree to infer the fork when direct hook information is missing.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4d7a60a-ad74-449d-9e1f-59d73cd3f78b","uuid":"93ccedc1-8ce0-424e-96a2-fc812bea8a7b"}
